    fullDescription: "One of the most original attractions on the whole Yucatán coast, MUSA — the Museo Subacuático de Arte — is a vast underwater sculpture museum lying on the seabed in the shallow waters between Cancún and Isla Mujeres. Conceived partly as art and partly as conservation, it comprises more than 500 life-size concrete statues created by sculptor Jason deCaires Taylor and others, deliberately sunk to form an artificial reef that draws marine life away from the region's fragile natural reefs and gives coral, sponges and fish a new home. The effect is genuinely haunting and beautiful: ranks of human figures — 'The Silent Evolution', a crowd of ordinary people cast from local residents — a Volkswagen Beetle, a house, a man at a desk, all gradually being colonised by coral and drifting with fish, the light filtering down over them. Because the galleries sit at depths of only a few metres, you don't have to be a diver to see them: snorkelling tours float you over the shallower installations, scuba divers get closer to the deeper pieces, and glass-bottom boats let non-swimmers peer down at the eerie tableau. Morning trips generally offer the calmest water and best visibility. Book a reputable operator, wear reef-safe sunscreen or a rash vest, and combine the excursion with a stop at Isla Mujeres for a rounded day on the water.",
