    fullDescription: "Taxis are plentiful in Cancún and useful for door-to-door trips, late nights or when you're carrying luggage or shopping, but they work differently from many places and it pays to understand the system. Crucially, Cancún taxis do not use meters — fares are based on zones and are, in theory, set by official tariffs, but in practice they're negotiable and drivers frequently quote inflated prices to tourists. The golden rule is therefore to always agree the fare in pesos before you get in, ideally knowing the rough going rate for your route in advance (your hotel can advise), and to have small peso notes ready, as drivers often claim to lack change. Prices are higher for taxis picked up outside Hotel Zone resorts than for those flagged on the street, and hotel-arranged taxis cost more still. Fares within the Hotel Zone are modest, but the strip-to-downtown run costs noticeably more — which is exactly why the cheap R-1 bus is such a better deal for that journey. Ride-hailing apps like Uber and DiDi do operate in Cancún and offer transparent, often cheaper pricing, but there's a long-running conflict with the powerful local taxi unions, so availability can be patchy, pickups near the airport and some hotels can be awkward or discouraged, and it's worth being discreet. For most short trips, agreeing a taxi fare upfront or simply taking the bus is easiest. Always settle the price before departing, carry small change, consider the apps for fairer fares, and use the bus for the downtown run.",
