    fullDescription: "Cancún can be surprisingly affordable or genuinely lavish depending entirely on how you travel, with a bigger gap between budget and luxury than most beach destinations because of the two very different worlds of the resort strip and the local downtown. As a rough guide per person per day, excluding flights: a budget traveller staying in a downtown hostel or guesthouse ($15–30), eating at taquerías and market cocinas, getting around on the R-1 bus and enjoying the free public beaches can manage on around $40–60. A mid-range visitor in a decent Hotel Zone hotel or a room-only resort ($120–200 for a double), mixing local and tourist restaurants, taking a couple of tours and the odd taxi, should budget roughly $120–200. A luxury or all-inclusive trip — a premium beachfront resort with everything bundled, spa treatments, fine dining and private tours — easily runs to $350 or more per person per day. The single biggest variable is accommodation and whether you go all-inclusive: an all-inclusive resort front-loads most of your food, drink and entertainment costs into one price, which can be great value for those who want to relax on-site, whereas staying room-only and eating out gives more freedom and, if you venture downtown, real savings. The other big costs are tours and activities (Chichén Itzá, the nature parks, diving), which add up quickly. To keep costs down, use the cheap local bus, eat where locals eat downtown, enjoy the free public beaches, and consider room-only over all-inclusive if you like exploring.",
