    fullDescription: "The biggest decision when planning a Cancún trip is whether to book an all-inclusive resort or a room-only hotel, and it shapes both your budget and the character of your holiday. All-inclusive resorts — a Cancún speciality — bundle your room, all meals and snacks, drinks (often including alcohol and sometimes premium brands), non-motorised watersports, entertainment and kids' clubs into a single upfront price, typically from around $200 a night for a couple at the mid-range up to $900 at the luxury end. The appeal is convenience and predictability: you pay once and can relax on-site without opening your wallet, which suits honeymooners, families and anyone who wants a stress-free week by the pool and beach. The downsides are that you're incentivised to stay put (so you miss the city's excellent independent restaurants and the real Mexico beyond the gates), the food and drink quality varies, and if you plan to be out exploring, touring and eating elsewhere much of the time, you're paying for meals you won't eat. Room-only or bed-and-breakfast stays, by contrast, cost less upfront (from around $60 for a modest hotel), give you the freedom and motivation to eat out at Cancún's varied restaurants and downtown taquerías, and reward the curious traveller — but you then budget separately for every meal, drink and activity. As a rule of thumb: choose all-inclusive if you want to switch off and stay on-resort; choose room-only if you intend to tour the ruins, cenotes and islands and sample the local food scene. Match the model to how you actually plan to spend your days.",
