    fullDescription: "Activities and tours are where a Cancún budget can balloon, so it helps to know the going rates. At the affordable end, the Mayan ruins are excellent value: entry to Tulum or Cobá is only around $6, and even Chichén Itzá, the flagship, is about $32 all-in for the site itself. Cenote swims are similarly cheap, roughly $5–25 depending on how developed the site is. Guided full-day tours that bundle transport, a guide, lunch and often a cenote — the easy way to see Chichén Itzá, Tulum or Cobá — run from about $45 to $120 depending on the itinerary and group size. At the premium end sit the famous nature parks: Xcaret, Xel-Há and Xplor cost roughly $110–170 for an all-inclusive day, with Xcaret's night show and Xel-Há's food-and-drinks inclusion adding value. Water activities like snorkelling the MUSA underwater museum start around $50, while two-tank dives run $90–130 and swim-with-dolphins experiences $99 and up. Nightlife has its own price tag: Coco Bongo's open-bar show ticket is around $95–160, and Party Center clubs charge $30–100 for cover or open-bar packages. Two money-saving tips matter most: book tours and park tickets online in advance, where prices are usually lower and slots guaranteed, and be extremely wary of anyone offering suspiciously cheap or 'free' tours, transfers or activities, as these are almost always timeshare-presentation traps that will cost you hours of hard-sell. Use reputable operators, book ahead, and factor these costs into your trip budget from the start.",
