    fullDescription: "Food prices in Cancún vary enormously depending on where you eat, arguably more than almost any other destination, because of the gulf between the tourist Hotel Zone and the local downtown. In the Hotel Zone, restaurants are priced for a captive, affluent resort market and often quote in US dollars: a main course at a mid-range restaurant runs $20–40, and the city's celebrated upscale venues — the lobster houses, steakhouses and dinner-show restaurants — can easily reach $60–140 per person with drinks. Cocktails and drinks on the strip are similarly inflated. Cross into downtown El Centro, however, and prices plummet to what Mexicans actually pay: tacos at a good taquería cost just $1–3 each, a hearty meal at a family cocina or market stall $6–12, and even sit-down local restaurants are a fraction of Hotel Zone rates, while the food is frequently more authentic and delicious. The lesson is clear: for the best value and the most genuine Mexican food, eat downtown or at least a street or two back from the main beachfront strip, and use the cheap R-1 bus to get there. Even within the Hotel Zone, seeking out the handful of more locally-oriented spots (like casual seafood shacks) beats the resort-district tourist traps. All-inclusive guests sidestep much of this by having meals bundled in, but miss the local food scene as a result. Tap water isn't drinkable, so budget for bottled water. Tipping of 10–15% is customary in restaurants. Eat local, drink bottled water, tip fairly, and your food budget stretches a very long way.",
